Convert the given data to SI units:
\[
\rho=935\ \text{kg/m}^3,\qquad \mu=0.00195\ \text{Pa·s},\qquad D=0.03\ \text{m}
\]
\[
Q=66\ \text{L/min}=0.0011\ \text{m}^3/\text{s}
\]

The mean velocity is
\[
V=\frac{Q}{\pi D^2/4}
=\frac{0.0011}{\pi(0.03)^2/4}
\approx1.556\ \text{m/s}.
\]

Thus,
\[
Re=\frac{\rho VD}{\mu}
=\frac{935(1.556)(0.03)}{0.00195}
\approx2.24\times10^4,
\]
so the flow is turbulent. For a smooth pipe, the Colebrook equation gives a Darcy friction factor \(f\approx0.0252\) (Blasius gives approximately \(0.0258\)).

The pressure gradient is therefore
\[
\frac{\Delta p}{L}=f\frac{\rho V^2}{2D}
\approx0.0252\frac{935(1.556)^2}{2(0.03)}
\approx951\ \text{Pa/m}.
\]

Converting using \(1\ \text{mm Hg}=133.322\ \text{Pa}\):
\[
951\ \frac{\text{Pa}}{\text{m}}
\times\frac{0.01\ \text{m}}{1\ \text{cm}}
\times\frac{1\ \text{mm Hg}}{133.322\ \text{Pa}}
\approx0.0713\ \frac{\text{mm Hg}}{\text{cm}}.
\]

This matches **(E)**. Response B’s answer, **(B)**, is wrong, and the gold answer is correct.

